Falsifies `system_tests::user::x86_port_tests::port_holder_transmits_then_a_non_holder_faults`.

Architecture: x86_64

The defect is the "optimisation" a reader of the lazy design is most likely to write: an early
return in `arch::segments::set_port_range_grant` when the incoming thread holds no port grant, on
the reasoning that a thread with no ports needs nothing installed for it. It is wrong because the
TSS bitmap is per core and outlives the thread that installed it: after the holder runs, the
bitmap permits COM1 and `iomap_base` points at it, and every later switch-in with `None` now
leaves both as they are. The non-holder inherits the holder's ports.

Milestone 299's own BUGS named this the load-bearing property ("a thread that holds a port
capability switching to one that does not must leave the TSS denying ports, or the second thread
inherits the first's access"), and this record is the first time it has been broken on purpose.
Before milestone 313's audit the test could not have gone red for it: the non-holder reported on a
rendezvous nobody was receiving, so an inherited grant hung the run.

The first, weaker defect tried is worth recording because it did NOT fire: removing only the
re-deny of the outgoing holder's bits leaves the bitmap permitting COM1 but still moves `iomap_base`
past the segment limit, and the CPU consults no bitmap at all then, so the non-holder still faults.
The hand-off is protected twice, and the early return is the defect that defeats both.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(msg[0], EVENT_FAULT, "a non-holder's `out` must fault, not be
permitted")`, holding `EVENT_EXIT` (2) where it wanted `EVENT_FAULT` (1): the non-holder's `out`
is permitted, it exits, and the supervisor's message is a clean exit. The holder's half of the test
(its word arrives) is unaffected by the patch and passes first.

Regenerated 2026-09-25 (UTC) against 53cb6d620, which landed
milestone 315 (a port revoke that reaches every core). That commit split `set_port_range_grant` into a switch-path wrapper and
`set_port_range_grant_on`, the body the revocation NMI also calls. The early return goes in the
wrapper, which is where the "optimisation" would be written: the switch path is the only caller that
passes `None` for a thread that simply holds nothing. Putting it in the shared body instead would
also disable the revoke and self-delete resets, a wider defect that proves less about this test.

Replayed 2026-10-03 (UTC), and the red is narrower than the prose above implies. `cargo xtask test
--arch x86_64 --test port_holder_transmits_then_a_non_holder_faults` boots the image twice. Under
the defect the direct-boot leg stays green (`test result: ok. 1 passed`) and the red comes only
from the real-firmware leg (QEMU q35 with OVMF), at `system_tests/src/user/x86_port_tests.rs:221`.
So this record is evidence for the test on that leg only. Why the direct boot does not see the
inherited grant is not known; `script/falsifications` counts a test selected once per boot as one
test since milestone 323 (the falsification record is incomplete in five ways), so this record is swept as `red` rather than `stale`.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/segments.rs b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/segments.rs
index efa61fbd7..dc9c76de7 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/segments.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/segments.rs
@@ -375,6 +375,10 @@ unsafe fn write_range_bits(iomap: *mut u8, base: u16, count: u16, deny: bool) {
 /// out of `schedule()`'s switch), for the price of a call and a compare on the common switch.
 #[cold]
 pub fn set_port_range_grant(grant: Option<(u16, u16)>) {
+    // A thread with no port capability needs nothing installed for it.
+    if grant.is_none() {
+        return;
+    }
     set_port_range_grant_on(crate::cpu::id(), grant);
 }
 
